Description:
  Changes a board

Usage:
  tasker board update <id-or-name> [options]

Arguments:
  <id-or-name>  Board (id or name)

Options:
  --name <name>                          New name
  --status-set <status-set>              New status sets of the board (id or name); replaces the current ones
  --column <column>                      Column, left to right: "Name=status,status" (statuses by id or name). A task moved into the column gets the first of its statuses that belongs to the task type's status set. Replaces all columns; a column with the name of an existing one stays the same column and keeps its query
  --column-query <column-query>          Query of a column, in addition to its statuses (AND): "Column:query", the same language as the query bar over task lists, for example "Review:.Field.Component == \"API\" | sort -.Field.Estimate". Once per column; the column is named as in --column. A task is in the column only if its status fits and it matches the query; a | sort in it orders the column. Replaces the query of the named column (without --column - of that column of the current board; other columns keep theirs); "Column:" removes it
  --expected-version <expected-version>  Fail if the entity was changed after this version (as shown by get). Default: the current version
  -?, -h, --help                         Show help and usage information
  -w, --workspace <workspace>            Folder with the workspace (data lives in <folder>/.tasker). Default: the current folder
  -p, --project <project>                Project (id or name) for commands inside a project. Default: the TASKER_PROJECT variable, or the only project of the workspace
  --json                                 Print the result as JSON
  -q, --quiet                            Print less: lists skip the first line with the number of found items ('Found N'), sync prints nothing unless something needs attention
  --no-wrap, --truncate                  Cut long lines of list output to the window width with an ellipsis instead of wrapping (default: on in a terminal and under watch - COLUMNS and LINES both set; off when redirected)
  --no-truncate                          Never cut lines of list output, even in a terminal
  --width                                Line width for --truncate in characters (minimum 20); 0 or 'auto' - the terminal width, 'off' - never cut. Default: the TASKER_WIDTH variable (off or 0 - never cut), else the terminal

